1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,300
Half the people who reviewed this free demo had already played it for eleven hours.
Not one of them quit inside ten minutes. Not one.

2
00:00:08,550 --> 00:00:13,089
And the people leaving negative reviews had played
longer than the people leaving positive ones.

3
00:00:16,939 --> 00:00:25,736
Guildrun is a PvE autobattler roguelike. You build a team,
you bend heroes out of their class, and you watch the fight resolve itself.

4
00:00:25,986 --> 00:00:34,887
Nearly two thousand reviews, ninety percent positive,
and about two and a half thousand people in it as I record this. That's the biggest demo I've taken apart.

5
00:00:35,137 --> 00:00:39,388
It's also the first one where the file list
told me the team had done this before.

6
00:00:39,638 --> 00:00:47,468
Start with the awkward part. This is an I L two C P P build,
so all the C sharp is fused into one Game Assembly file.

7
00:00:47,718 --> 00:00:52,936
In the last teardown that was a dead end.
There's no folder of package names to read.

8
00:00:53,186 --> 00:01:01,983
Except it isn't quite a dead end.
Any assembly that shipped with embedded resources leaves its name behind in a data file, and Steam lists those.

9
00:01:02,233 --> 00:01:06,536
Twenty four names came out.
Here's what a team that has shipped before installs.

10
00:01:06,786 --> 00:01:16,157
Sentry, with six assemblies and a native crash handler behind it.
They've shipped a patch every single week since January. That's what makes that survivable.

11
00:01:16,407 --> 00:01:24,237
S Q Lite, because the demo alone has three hundred relics and a hundred items.
At some point that stops being a pile of JSON files.

12
00:01:24,487 --> 00:01:33,284
N Log for structured logging. R three and System Reactive for event streams.
Threading Channels for the producer-consumer work.

13
00:01:33,534 --> 00:01:41,495
And Z Link, which exists for one reason: doing LINQ without allocating.
You install that when you've been bitten by garbage collection hitches.

14
00:01:41,745 --> 00:01:48,086
None of that is exciting.
It's the stuff you only know to install because something went wrong on the last project.

15
00:01:48,336 --> 00:01:56,636
Compare the solo demo I took apart two episodes ago.
Its entire third party list was one entry: Discord rich presence.

16
00:01:56,886 --> 00:02:03,175
Neither is wrong. But if you're about to start your second project,
that list up there is a shopping list.

17
00:02:03,425 --> 00:02:11,020
Audio is F MOD with Resonance, same as the last one.
Networking is nanosockets, a thin U D P layer.

18
00:02:11,270 --> 00:02:13,667
Then the number I stared at for a while.

19
00:02:13,917 --> 00:02:18,952
The demo is ten and a half gigabytes on disk.
The download is one point one seven.

20
00:02:19,202 --> 00:02:25,230
That's an eighty nine percent saving.
Which means the files sitting on your drive are barely compressed at all.

21
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:31,534
My first thought was that somebody forgot the import settings.
My second thought was to read the store page.

22
00:02:31,784 --> 00:02:39,301
Minimum requirements: fifteen gigabytes of storage,
and a line that says H D D okay, no S S D required.

23
00:02:39,551 --> 00:02:48,922
Uncompressed assets cost disk and save C P U. On a hard drive with no decompression to do,
they load faster. My read is that this is deliberate.

24
00:02:49,172 --> 00:02:56,349
They spent ten gigabytes of somebody's drive to keep the game playable on old hardware.
Tell me in the comments if I've got that backwards.

25
00:02:56,599 --> 00:03:05,265
There are also twenty two scenes in here.
The last two games I took apart shipped one each and built everything at runtime. This one is authored in the editor.

26
00:03:05,515 --> 00:03:10,707
So the engineering is careful.
The fight in the reviews is about something else entirely.

27
00:03:10,957 --> 00:03:17,167
Performance barely comes up. Ten mentions positive,
two negative. Nobody is complaining about the build.

28
00:03:17,417 --> 00:03:25,247
They're complaining about balance. Certain combinations only,
dead heroes, and a random draw that decides your run before you play it.

29
00:03:25,497 --> 00:03:33,327
And that's why the negative reviewers had fifteen hours in.
You don't find a balance ceiling in an hour. You find it after you've mastered the thing.

30
00:03:33,577 --> 00:03:37,698
Which is a very different problem from the
demos where people quit in the first hour.

31
00:03:37,948 --> 00:03:40,240
Here's the whole teardown on one card.

32
00:03:40,490 --> 00:03:50,514
Unity, I L two C P P, C sharp. A stack full of things you only reach for the second time around.
Ten gigabytes of deliberately uncompressed art.

33
00:03:50,764 --> 00:03:55,015
Worth a dip, and be careful,
because eleven hours is the median.

34
00:03:55,265 --> 00:04:03,095
If you're starting your next project, take the boring half of that list.
Crash reporting on day one is the cheapest thing you will ever install.

35
00:04:03,345 --> 00:04:13,578
That's my read from the outside. If you made this and I got something wrong,
say so and I'll pin the correction. Next time, another demo, another file list. This is Demo Dip.
